------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Concurrency
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

ControlState lock discipline (super_control_state.go):

  - `mu sync.RWMutex` guards the peers map, the parameters, the revision,
    and the publish hook.
  - All five mutating entry points (Register, DeletePeer, UpdateParameters,
    Report, SweepTimeouts) acquire `s.mu.Lock()`, mutate, then RELEASE
    BEFORE calling `s.emit(...)` or `s.publish(...)`. Verified by grep:
    every `s.emit`/`s.publish` call site sits strictly after
    `s.mu.Unlock()`. NO publish under lock. (ref: state:92-94,
    state:128-130, state:156-166, state:202-205, state:247-250)
  - `snapshotLocked(...)` iterates `s.peers` and clones each slice/map
    before releasing the lock, so no map is mutated while the snapshot
    is being built — no map iteration races.
  - `SweepTimeouts` builds the `events []mtypes.ControlV2PeerChangePayload`
    slice under the write lock (collecting names of to-be-removed peers),
    releases the lock, then iterates the slice to emit. The map delete
    happens under the lock; the slice iteration that calls `s.emit`
    happens outside the lock. Safe.
  - `DeletePeer` performs the map delete under the lock, releases it,
    then emits `peer_gone`. Single revision bump per actual removal.
  - `SetPublishForTest` (state:285) takes the lock to swap the hook.
    Documented as a test seam; production wiring installs the hook
    exactly once at startup before any traffic flows
    (main_super.go:319), so production code never races the swap.

Event hub slow-subscriber eviction + Close idempotency
(super_control_events.go):

  - Hub.Publish (events:144-171) snapshots subscribers under the lock,
    releases the lock, then iterates calling each sub's `tryDeliver`.
    The lock is NEVER held while writing to a sub channel.
  - `tryDeliver` (events:461-470) uses a non-blocking send with
    a `default:` branch that calls `sub.markEvicted(ErrSlowSubscriber)`;
    the publisher NEVER blocks on a slow consumer.
  - Hub.Close (events:294-310) is wrapped in `closeOnce.Do(...)` so it
    is idempotent. It snapshots subs under the lock, releases the lock,
    then cancels and marks each sub. NO double-close.
  - Subscriber.Close (events:422-424) is idempotent (just calls
    `s.cancel()`).
  - markEvicted uses CompareAndSwap to avoid double-closing the done
    channel; markEvictedIfNotSet provides the same once-protection.
  - Hub mutex is released BEFORE the slow-subscriber send; even if a
    sub's buffer fills, the publisher path is bounded to O(N) channel
    tries, never blocks waiting on a sub.
  - The replay ring is bounded by `capacity` and silently evicts the
    oldest entry when full (events:115-135). No memory leak.

Runtime apply serialization (device/super_http_runtime.go):

  - `runtime.apply sync.Mutex` is acquired at the top of
    `applySnapshot` (runtime:136) and held across the call into
    `device.applySuperHTTPSnapshot(snapshot)` which mutates
    `device.peers.IDMap`, `device.graph`, and the cached endpoint list.
  - The runtime registers TWO goroutines that both invoke
    `applySnapshot`: the Sync SSE goroutine and the Sync poll-goroutine
    (and the initial Register path at start). The `apply` mutex
    guarantees sequential application, addressing the task 4 notepad
    concern: "T10's runtime MUST serialize snapshot application to
    peers/graph." Implemented.
  - `Start` uses `once.Do(...)` so it's idempotent against accidental
    double-start.
  - `MarkReady` (runtime:51-56) sends on a buffered channel of cap 1
    with a `default:` non-blocking branch — MarkReady is safe to call
    more than once without ever blocking the bind-ready path.
  - `run` (runtime:60-94) waits for either the ready signal or ctx
    cancellation before doing any network I/O. On ctx-done it returns
    immediately — no goroutine is left leaked if the device closes
    before the bind listen port is ready.
  - `run` registers Sync + reportLoop, then `wg.Wait()` — both
    goroutines terminate when ctx cancels, run() returns, the `done`
    channel is closed via `defer close(runtime.done)`.
  - reportLoop uses time.NewTimer + a `<-ctx.Done()` selection; on
    cancel it stops/drains the timer before returning (no leaked
    timer goroutine).
  - `setCandidates` and `refreshSTUN` use `runtime.mu` (separate RWMutex)
    protecting the candidates slice. Report loop reads via `RLock` and
    clones the slice before passing to Report — no slice sharing races.

STUN manager pending-map lifecycle (device/super_stun.go):

  - Pending map `map[[stun.TransactionIDSize]byte]chan stunResult` is
    guarded by `manager.mu sync.Mutex`.
  - `request(...)` inserts the response channel under the lock,
    sends the BindingRequest on the WireGuard bind, then waits on a
    context-cancellable select for either the response or the timeout.
    The deferred `delete` removes the entry EVEN ON timeout / error
    paths, so no stale entries leak into the map.
  - `HandlePacket` validates magic cookie + BindingSuccess + Fingerprint,
    looks up the pending entry under the lock, sends to the buffered
    channel (non-blocking), and returns true so the receive path knows
    it consumed a STUN frame. Bytes stay out of the WireGuard dissector.
  - The pending response channel has buffer 1, so `HandlePacket` cannot
    leak the publisher goroutine if the request already timed out.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. HTTP boundary (super_control_auth.go, super_control_http.go,
                 device/super_http_client.go)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Body size cap BEFORE hashing:

  - Verify (auth:296-306) wraps `r.Body` in
    `http.MaxBytesReader(nil, r.Body, ControlAuthMaxBodyBytes)` (1 MiB),
    then `io.ReadAll(capped)`. The cap is installed BEFORE the SHA-256
    digest, so an oversized body is rejected before any non-trivial
    work is done.
  - `*http.MaxBytesError` is caught and translated into the typed
    `ErrControlAuthBodyTooLarge`, which surfaces as HTTP 413 (the only
    "client-fixable" auth status). The cap value is intentionally not
    echoed back to the caller.

Uniform auth errors:

  - All auth sub-checks (missing header, invalid NodeID, unknown node,
    invalid timestamp, body too large, invalid signature, replay,
    malformed signature) return `*ControlAuthError{Code: <sentinel>}`.
  - `Error()` returns the constant `uniformControlAuthMessage =
    "control auth failed"`. No sub-check is distinguishable from the
    outside.
  - `writeAuthError` (http:237-242) calls `http.Error(w,
    "control auth failed", status)` — the body never reveals which
    sub-check failed and never contains key material.
  - The mapping is uniform: only 413 for body too large; everything
    else is 401.

ETag/304 correctness:

  - `handleSnapshot` (http:180-194) computes `snap.ETag()` (a
    `"rev-<n>"` string), sets it on the response, and compares it to
    `r.Header.Get("If-None-Match")`. On match, writes 304 with no body.
    On mismatch, writes the snapshot.
  - Client-side `Snapshot()` (client:99-133) reads the cached snapshot
    under lock, then sets `If-None-Match` on the outgoing request.
    On `StatusNotModified`, returns the cached snapshot unchanged with
    `false` (no apply needed); on non-200, returns an error
    (`fmt.Errorf("snapshot: %s", resp.Status)`).

No 404-as-not-modified:

  - Status 404 is never treated as "snapshot unchanged". The client
    treats `404` exactly like any other non-200/non-304 (an error).

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. SSE cancellation (super_control_events.go, super_control_http.go)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Hub closure:

  - ControlEventHub.Close (events:294-310) is wrapped in `closeOnce.Do`,
    so multiple Close calls are safe.
  - On close, the hub marks itself closed, snapshots subs, then cancels
    each sub and calls `markEvictedIfNotSet(ErrHubClosed)`. The lock is
    NOT held across the cancel-and-evict.

Renderer termination:

  - `SSERenderer.run` (events:536-568) selects on `ctx.Done()`,
    `sub.Done()`, the heartbeat ticker, and the per-sub events channel.
    Any one of those terminating causes the pump goroutine to return.
  - Heartbeats (`: heartbeat`) are written under `r.deliverMu` so a
    slow heartbeat write cannot interleave with an event write.
  - Per-event writes go through `writeEventLocked` and write failures
    are recorded via atomic `setWriteErr` — failures terminate the
    pump without leaking.

Goroutine leak audit:

  - server side (http:206-232): `handleEvents` writes a 200 + initial
    SSE framing, then `defer renderer.Close()` is registered. When
    `r.Context()` cancels (client disconnect), the handler returns.
    `renderer.Close()` calls `r.cancel()` and `r.wg.Wait()` — the
    renderer's pump goroutine has multiple termination signals
    (`ctx.Done()`, `sub.Done()`), so it returns promptly.
  - The runtime shutdown contract (main_super.go:446-507) shuts down
    the hub BEFORE calling `http.Server.Shutdown(ctx)`, then
    `r.hub.Close()` cancels every subscriber FIRST, so every
    in-flight SSE handler observes a done subscriber/render and
    returns promptly. After `Shutdown(ctx)` returns or times out,
    a `Server.Close()` force-close kicks in to unblock any handler
    that is still sitting on `<-r.Context().Done()`.

Client side (device/super_http_client.go):

  - `sseParseTracked` (client:326-339) spawns a parser goroutine
    that pushes events into a buffered channel. When ctx cancels,
    the body close triggers scanner EOF and the goroutine returns.
    The drain loop returns when the parser completes.
  - `Sync` (client:352-424) runs SSE reconnects in a `for { select }`
    loop. Per-iteration context cancellation cleanly terminates
    both the Events call and the parser goroutine; the loop returns
    on `<-ctx.Done()`. `pollLoop` is a separate goroutine that
    terminates on its parent `pollCtx` (cancelled by Sync's defer).
  - No goroutine in Sync/Events/sseParse leaks on normal
    cancellation — every goroutine has at least two termination
    paths (ctx done + channel closed/drained).

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. Secret handling (mtypes/http_control.go + review of all sources)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Control PSKey in JSON:

  - mtypes/http_control.go:257 declares `PSKey string `json:"-"` for
    `ControlV2Peer`. JSON marshalling NEVER emits the field.
  - mtypes/http_control.go:434-437 declares ALL four fields of
    `SuperNodeV2Ref` (`APIUrl`, `APIPrefix`, `NodeID`, `ControlPSKey`)
    with `json:"-"` so even a stray serialisation can't leak the key.
  - Defense-in-depth: `super_control_state.go:261` actively sets
    `peer.PSKey = ""` in `snapshotLocked` before the peer is added
    to the per-request snapshot — the field is zeroed regardless of
    JSON tag.

Control PSKey in errors:

  - `super_control_auth.go`: the produced `*ControlAuthError` has only
    a Code sentinel; the public Error() string is the constant
    "control auth failed". No key material in errors.
  - `super_control_http.go`: `writeAuthError` writes the constant
    "control auth failed" body.

Control PSKey in logs:

  - No `log.X` / `Errorf` in the super_*.go or device/super_*.go
    production sources includes any PSKey material. The two
    Errorf sites in super_http_runtime.go (lines 77 and 86) log
    only the err returned by Register/Sync — neither includes
    the key.

Control PSKey in URLs:

  - `grep -rn "r.URL.Query\|FormValue\|ParseForm"` against the HTTP
    boundary returns ZERO matches in production paths. No URL query
    string ever carries a credential.
  - All credentials are in the HMAC headers (`X-EG-Signature`) plus
    the signed body, never in the URL.

Control PSKey in SSE data:

  - SSE events emitted via `Publish` carry `ControlV2PeerChangePayload`
    (NodeID + NodeName only) or `ControlV2Parameters`. Neither carries
    a control PSKey.
  - Snapshot events produced by `EventType` carry `Parameters`, never
    the per-peer control keys.
  - The renderer (events:572-611) serialises `ev.Data` via
    `json.Marshal(ev.Data)`. Since `ev.Data` is one of the typed
    payloads above, the rendered JSON cannot leak a PSKey.

  Note on `info.PSKey` in device/super_http_runtime.go:220-224:
  this is the per-Edge INTER-EDGE PSK (pairwise symmetric key for
  WireGuard between two Edges), NOT the Super-control authentication
  PSKey. The plan explicitly permits inter-Edge PSKs in snapshots when
  `usePSKForInterEdge=true` ("No exposure of an Edge's Super-control
  `PSKey` in v2 responses... only existing pairwise inter-Edge PSKs
  may be returned when enabled."). Read 220-224 with care to confirm
  this is consumed only for `peer.SetPSK(...)`, never re-emitted.

